at he wasn't a complicated  personality because he was very much so  offstage he could be Moody and profaned  he had a habit of flying into red rages  that he forgot all about the next day  and he knew how to hold a grudge I got a  simple rule about everybody he told the  journalist if you don't treat me right  shame on you but that's merely to say  that arms  as a human being as flawed as the next  man and if there's anything that I  learned in the process of writing Pop's  it is that Louis Armstrong's flaws were  few and forgivable in return for his  unswerving dedication to his art he knew  true happiness and shared it  unstintingly with his fellow men not  long before he died he told a friend  that my whole life has been happiness  through all the misfortunes etc I did  not plan anything life was there for me  and I accepted it in life whatever came  out has been beautiful to me and I love  everybody I can't top that so I'll let  pops have the last word  well now the floor is yours I would be  more than happy to answer any questions  that you might have about Louis  Armstrong or about my book if you'll  make your way to the microphone I'll do  my best questions and thanks so much for  doing the book one is in terms of his  early life there's a book called Louis  Armstrong's in New Orleans it's sort of  a set in context what what did you bring  new information Lee about his early life  that we don't have from other books not  that's one in the second how did he  react to all the criticism that he got  from other jazz musicians sort of in the  younger newer wave and the 60s you know  the and who didn't like his you know  sort of what they viewed as some Uncle  Tom ISM of the stage act how did he  react to that a whole bunch of questions  there  Thomas Brothers Louis Armstrong's New  Orleans is the most important scholarly  monograph ever written about Armstrong  it is a thorough inspection strong early  years I think beyond what brothers did  which was to look at 