 the bebop era did not find funny  they felt uncomfortable about it and  they criticized him about it Dizzy  Gillespie most prominently and I think  most sharply I think they just didn't  take him in historical perspective and  some of them lived long enough to change  their tune including I'm happy to say  Dizzy Gillespie who in his autobiography  written at the end of his life  apologized to Armstrong for having  described him as an uncle  Tom in fact I will quote Gillespie  verbatim because these words have stuck  in my mind he said hell I had my own way  of timing so I think now that we are as  far beyond Armstrong's life as Gillespie  was beyond the beginnings of it we can  see him as he was as a figure from  history and as someone who's art and I  think whose personality is of permanent  interest in permanently contemporary  even if there are aspects of it that we  wouldn't especially want to see at the  movies today let us say insurance great  work now yes sir thank you for your talk  thanks for this lovely book I imagine  for a serious critic like yourself it's  hard to sustain focus on and produce an  objective book on a subject that is so  utterly loveable and who you obviously  loved and you pulled it off so well I  mean I don't pretend to be visted  objectivity sounds you know like  something that came out of the icebox  and there's a sense in which I'm not an  even slightly objective about Louis  Armstrong it was a great man but I tried  to write as a scholar would write and  you know to to do what he said on more  than one occasion he would want a  biographer to do which was to tell the  whole story of his life to tell them  well this book is scholarly and it is  warm so it's very nice so I'm going to  ask you a very specific question because  it's so thoroughly agreeable you really  have to work to find like a disagreement  about a choice of composition or  something I'll ask you that and it has  to do about latter day Armstrong and you  can answer it and perhaps talk a little  bit about Louis the last decade or so in  how creative he was and the charges that  you're familiar with them that last  decade of him as a musician in this book  you refer to the real ambassadors as too  earnest for its own good  yeah that's for everybody who doesn't  know the album it's a musical that Dave  and I own a Brubeck wrote in which it  was only done on record and Armstrong  appears in it performs in it in my  opinion now they say I love I look like  God is one of those beautiful well that  he says ever done beautiful son on race  relations it's just you know he was  supposedly interiors at the end of it  and and of course one of the  some real ambassadors is in my appendix  of my 30 favorite Armstrong I'm  recording summer song which is also  beautiful but there's a whole bunch of  other jamming songs in so anyways you  you make that remark if you've been  comment on that but just comment on that  last decade and whether maybe his  creativity was limited by the fact that  he was working all these years of the  Joe Glaser who may not have been as  plugged into tapping artists I'm trying  to genius until the end let's let's talk  a little bit about that Joe Glaser was  Armstrong's manager from 1935 until  blazars death about a year and a half  before Armstrong died Glaser was to a  great extent responsible for bringing  Armstrong to a wider popular audience  but as I say repeatedly in my book  people who knew Armstrong who knew  Glaser who played with Armstrong who  worked in blazers office they all said  the same thing which was that Armstrong  went where Glaser told him to go but  when he got there he played what he  wanted to play Armstrong is not a man  who really thinks of himself in a  self-conscious way as an artist and he  knew he was an artist on only one  occasion did I ever find him talking  about how jazz was art that's in the  book but he knew what he was but he  thought of himself as an entertainer and  he didn't think there was anything  incompatible between being an artist and  being an entertainer and I think he got  as much pleasure out of making people  laugh as he did thrilling them and I say  this somewhere in the book we must take  great artists as they were rather than  imagining what we wish they were and  tried to take them that way instead the  music that Armstrong record in the last  10 years of his life is spottier than  the music you recorded in the 20s or 30s  but the high points are as high as  anything that he did at any time in his  life summer song with Brubeck the  amazing recording of Black and Tans  fantasy that he made was Duke Ellington  and it was 61 and let us say it Hello  Dolly which is a great pop record but  also a great jazz record it really is  you know I think I wouldn't want if it  were left up to me  I wouldn't want only to have the music  of the last ten years of Armstrong's  life to listen to but if that's all I  had I'd take it and be glad who's who's  up next I just have a quick question  yeah  you spoke about the five or six hundred  tapes that he made at any time is Louis  Armstrong's home in New York a library  at this pointer is it open to the pilot  glad you asked me that not only is it a  museum but I'm speaking there on  Saturday they're throwing a book party  for me and I'm going to give a shorter  version of this talk in Armstrong's own  home it was opened up it was restored to  its original condition a few years ago  and opened as a museum and I must tell  you that if you have the slightest  interest in Louis Armstrong you must go  to this place because the walls breathe  his essence it is one of the most  amazing house museums in the world thank  God that it was preserved and and is the  way it is so I'll be thinking of you all  on Saturday afternoon when I when I go  there and speak there all the tapes okay  there are 650 reel-to-reel tapes  Armstrong I'll tell you the whole story  Armstrong bought one of the first  commercial tape recorders ever sold in  the United States in 1947 and Bing  Crosby bought one of the others by the  way and at first he bought it to record  his shows so that he could listen to  them and perfect them a reminder that  Armstrong is in fact a true artist and  self aware artist but like everybody  else who first got their hands on a tape  recorder when tape recorders renew he  started playing with it and pretty soon  he figured out that he could just turn  it on backstage in the dressing room or  in his hotel room or or wherever and let  it run and he saved the tapes they were  stored in the attic of the house they  made their way into the Armstrong  archives I was the first biographer to  get to listen to them and now I will  tell you a story about one of them  because I think it exemplifies what they  tell us they tell us important facts  there's one type where Armstrong talks  about his run-in with the mobsters in  Chicago and names name  something he didn't do anywhere else but  some of the tapes are just fun so I  mentioned this in passing in the talk  but I'll tell you a little bit more  about it there is a tape my guess is  that it was recorded sometime in the 50s  in a hotel room somewhere in the world I  have no idea where it's 5:00 in the  morning Louis Armstrong is trying to get  his wife Lucille to go to bed with him  and she's really not interested in this  at 5:00 in the morning and declines with  thanks and he says well it's your duty  to do that it's your duty to keep the  horn percolating well this didn't amuse  her one bit and then she looks around  and you can almost hear the double take  on the tape she sees the tape recorder  is running and she says to him you turn  that off in fact you erase off some of  that stuff and he says I can't do that  this is for posterity okay alright flash  forward 50 years Here I am you know that  the scholar with the headphones on  sitting in the Armstrong archives at  Queens College and I'm listening to this  tape and suddenly it hits me my god I'm  posterity he saved it for me and he he  saved it for all of us because he knew  that he was after all Louis Armstrong  and he wanted us to know what he was  like and those tapes tell us that better  than anything other than his art can  possibly tell us yes sir  congratulations on your nomination for  the n-double-a-cp  I'm very proud um to sort of piggyback  on that that theme could you really I  guess share with us the true impact of  what he did for race relations in the  United States yes I liked very much like  to talk about I guess my next question  is how did he really grapple with the  criticism because one of his other  admirers is Ralph Ellison you know  during the late 60s when he was being  criticized along with Louis Armstrong he  really um really took it bad when you  know the black Nash was really tolling  he was on the job well I mean  essentially Armstrong grappled with the  criticism by living his life I mean  occasionally he would he would try to  set the record straight and talk about  the things he had done but he didn't  lose a whole lot of sleep over what  people said about him even though he  didn't  like it he wasn't the kind of man to  lose a whole lot of sleep about anything  that anybody said about him now as far  as what he contributed to race relations  I think I can answer that question best  by telling a story I come from a town in  southeast Missouri  it's called Saxton none of you will have  heard of it  my father witnessed a lynching it was  that kind of town Louis Armstrong was  probably excuse me for many people in  that town Louis Armstrong was probably  the first black person I ever took  seriously as an artist as a man and I  think that by reaching out through his  art and his personality he bridged gaps  then no one else could have bridged  either I'm been affected by this and  that  I think meant as much I talked about  this in in less personal terms in the  book but I think that that is meant as  much to the public at large in the 50s  and 60s as was as much of a contribution  to racial justice and racial relations  as anything that anybody was doing at  that time simply by his own personal  example that's the best way I can answer  that question and I saw that at first  hand heavily other questions the  microphone awaits you and so do I but if  I we have somebody is rising to go to  the microphone so I'll give you a minute  there while I recapture my composure I  do you address in the book his standing  as a jazz icon of the black community  with black Americans versus white is  there a difference I mean where does his  standing is huge with white Americans of  area I know that but is his is he  considered like the pinnacle what he is  now  yeah but I never thought look part of  what my book is is what scholars call a  reception history which is an attempt to  try to describe how Armstrong was  written about and how people responded  to him throughout the course of his  career and like every other jazz  musician Armstrong ceased to a great  extent to be popular in the black  community because it lost interest in  jazz is a community it's really troubled  him there's a funny story I tell in the  book which I really can't repeat here  about his going to a gig by Louie Jordan  and his was halfway empty and he just  couldn't believe what had happened Miles  Davis talked the same way in the 60s  about how how the racial composition of  jazz audiences had changed and yes he  was troubled by it on the other hand  again jazz is now a historical music a  historical event rather than a popular  music and  I think that nowadays pretty much  everybody sees Armstrong in the same way  as the central figure in the history of  jazz in the 20th century there's no  racial differential there he's is  important to everybody who cares about  jazz no matter who they are that's  something that I would say has happened  in the last quarter century there are a  lot of factors that have to do that the  advocacy of Wynton Marsalis has been a  very important part of making  Armstrong's achievement known especially  to younger people but part of it is just  that jazz is now itself the 20th century  jazz the 20th century is over and we  look upon it as a closed event and we  know how important Armstrong was that's  the best way I can answer your question  jazz clubs in New York and Lee but maybe  that's a very small well New York is  only a very small part of the audience  for jazz thank heavens yes I hear there  are jazz clubs in Washington as well I  want to ask you about your other writing  especially your writing in The Wall  Street Journal which I read I'm sure  other people read your columns and what  your sense of your sensibility and  sensitivity and writing for the journal  brings not to a wider public audience  but to the audience and people who read  those kinds of writings in the  publication that is perhaps growing and  influenced for the kinds of things that  you're bringing or whether you feel that  way or not I don't know how to answer  that I don't really I don't really think  about it that way I mean how I think of  myself what I do I am a specialist who  trance tries to translate the knowledge  of a specialist into generalist language  I mean I can and have in the past done  so written for scholarly audiences but  I'm not really interested in doing that  I'm interested in taking what I know  about the arts and I do write about all  the arts and I'm not just jazz not just  drama and and try to communicate this  knowledge to try to be a teacher and  also maybe above all is a critic to try  to communicate enthusiasm because I  think that is the  important thing that a credit can do I  mean the biggest thing that critic does  is to say I saw this and you should too  and to be persuasive about it and for me  to be able to do that in the Wall Street  Journal which is now the daily paper  with the largest circulation in 